**Read:** [FiPy — conservative finite-volume modelling](../sources/FiPy-Conservative-Finite-Volume-Methods.lingua#r_5C155DKGJ5WYGV18EJ1SC41G0Q), verification and examples; [Convecção e métodos numéricos — Maliska, UFSC](../sources/Conveccao-and-Numerical-Methods-Maliska-UFSC.lingua#r_71K7JMRA8M0N3Y8EKMZJT9NGQ7), discretization and solution methods; [Guarded-hot-plate evidence — NIST SP 260-201](../sources/Guarded-Hot-Plate-and-SRM-1450e-NIST.lingua#r_1PP28H0Y6N7RVY89W9HSQY18E4), the distinction between a numerical correction and measurement evidence.

Start with a problem having a known solution or independently constructed benchmark. Check units, sign, conservation, symmetry, constant/zero limits and convergence order. A small residual shows that an algebraic system was solved; it does not prove that the correct physical equation or boundary was discretized.

Refine space and time separately, then together under a stated relation. Keep the observation time, geometry, interface placement and reported quantity fixed. A changing numerical peak at an ideal singularity may require a different physical or averaged observable rather than a false convergence claim.

Compare two implementations that do not share the same critical derivation or code. Preserve uncertainty and model discrepancy after numerical errors become small. A verified thermal model still needs suitable measurements, material properties and boundary evidence before it supports a real design claim.

